  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule. Both exist, the rule is enabled, and its first run finished with status `ok`. I couldn't look at the actual data: Kibana's console proxy is turned off here, so I never saw the date range or the response-code values. The dashboard's default time range (last 7 days) may need changing if the sample data falls outside it.\n\n**1. What the data holds**\nThe data view is `kibana_sample_data_logs`, with `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request, with these main fields:\n- **Request:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`.\n- **Result:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text with a `.keyword` version) and `bytes`.\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`.\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`.\n- **Other:** `tags`, plus a computed `hour_of_day` field.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-8ccee5e9 Web logs overview\"** (id `7f802b8f-7f13-44a5-90a8-8dbebbd61393`) has 8 panels:\n- Four single-number panels: total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average bytes per request, and count of 5xx errors.\n- A line chart of requests over time, one line per response code.\n- A donut chart of requests by response code.\n- Two tables: the top 10 URLs (with request count and total bytes) and the top 10 source countries (with requests and unique visitors).\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-8ccee5e9 5xx spike\"** (id `9bdc7448-f0bd-4f35-8725-9d88a3f81df6`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on the sample-logs data view. It filters with `response.keyword : 5*` and runs every minute.\n- It fires when the count is **greater than 10** within a **5-minute** window.\n- I set it to count every matching request in the window each time, including ones an earlier check already counted. This way it measures the true 5-minute total.\n- It has no notification actions attached, so it will create alerts but won't send any messages. Add a connector if you want notifications.",
